examples.question_text:
Find the exact values of $\operatorname{cosec}\alpha$, $\sec\alpha$ and $\cot\alpha$ for the triangle.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=7mm,draw=black,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,0)--(4,0)--(4,3)--cycle;
\draw (3.7,0)--(3.7,0.3)--(4,0.3);
\path (2,0) node[below] {$8$};
\path (4,1.5) node[right] {$6$};
\path (2,1.5) node[above left] {$10$};
\path (0.65,0.25) node {$\alpha$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
(a) Find the opposite side using Pythagoras' theorem.

$\begin{align*}\text{Opposite}&=\sqrt{26^2-10^2}\\&=\sqrt{576}\\&=24\end{align*}$

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=7mm,draw=black,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,0)--(2,0)--(2,4.8)--cycle;
\draw (1.7,0)--(1.7,0.3)--(2,0.3);
\path (1,0) node[below] {$10$};
\path (2,2.4) node[right] {$24$};
\path (1,2.4) node[above left] {$26$};
\path (0.45,0.4) node {$\theta$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$\begin{align*}\operatorname{cosec}\theta&=\dfrac{\text{hyp}}{\text{opp}}\\&=\dfrac{26}{24}\\&=\dfrac{13}{12}\end{align*}$

(b) Find the hypotenuse using Pythagoras' theorem.

$\begin{align*}\text{Hypotenuse}&=\sqrt{9^2+40^2}\\&=\sqrt{1681}\\&=41\end{align*}$

$\begin{align*}\sec\beta&=\dfrac{\text{hyp}}{\text{adj}}\\&=\dfrac{41}{40}\end{align*}$

On the unit circle, $r=1$, so $\sec\theta=\dfrac1x$, $\operatorname{cosec}\theta=\dfrac1y$ and $\cot\theta=\dfrac xy$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=12mm,y=12mm,draw=black,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw[->] (-1.4,0)--(1.7,0);
\draw[->] (0,-1.4)--(0,1.5);
\draw (0,0) circle (1);
\fill (0,1) circle (1.2pt);
\fill (1,0) circle (1.2pt);
\fill (0,-1) circle (1.2pt);
\path (1.7,0) node[right] {$x$};
\path (0,1.5) node[above] {$y$};
\path (0,1) node[above left] {$(0,1)$};
\path (1,0) node[above right] {$(1,0)$};
\path (0,-1) node[below left] {$(0,-1)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

(a) At $270^\circ$, $(x,y)=(0,-1)$. The denominator in $\sec270^\circ=\dfrac1x$ is zero, so secant is undefined.

(b) At $360^\circ$, $(x,y)=(1,0)$. The denominator in $\operatorname{cosec}360^\circ=\dfrac1y$ is zero, so cosecant is undefined.

(c) At $\dfrac\pi2$, $(x,y)=(0,1)$.

$\cot\dfrac\pi2=\dfrac01=0$.

Use the coordinate definition here: tangent is undefined, so its reciprocal expression cannot be used.

(d) At $0$, $(x,y)=(1,0)$. The denominator in $\cot0=\dfrac xy$ is zero, so cotangent is undefined.
